# Religious Endowments
Tamil title: அற நிலையங்கள்

On 24 March 1949, at a meeting held under the auspices of the Valluvar Association at Kothaval Chavadi, Madras, Arignar Anna delivered this speech in support of the Hindu Religious Endowments Protection Bill. Mr A. S. Duraisamy presided.

Beloved Chairman and comrades!

I am very happy that this meeting has been convened in this commercial quarter. You are merchants. Therefore, no one can come here, sell you spoiled goods, and simply go away. You weigh the goods brought to you and buy them only after deciding whether they are useful for your trade. In the same way, you must weigh everything spoken among you and everything that reaches your ears, and accept only what is sound.

You know that a mistaken impression has spread among some people that we of the Dravidar Kazhagam oppose everything the Congress says. It is wrong to say that we, whose purpose is reform, would oppose every measure and every law.

The orthodox Vaidyanatha Iyer and Varadachari oppose the Hindu Religious Endowments Protection Bill. Varadachari and Vaidyanatha Iyer are its hereditary enemies. Some Congressmen themselves oppose in the Legislature this Bill introduced by Congressmen. We, who are arrested and thrown into prison as Dravidar Kazhagam members, support it. The reason for our support is our impartial outlook. Varadachari and Vaidyanatha Iyer oppose it because of orthodox fanaticism. We do not possess that mean-spirited mania. Therefore, whether Congressmen invite us or not, we support the Bill.

What do we oppose? We oppose Brahmin deception, their domination, Brahminism, and their habit of sowing enmity. From the beginning, we of the Dravidar Kazhagam have condemned not Brahmins as persons, but the Brahminism dwelling among them. Present-day Brahminism supports the heads of monasteries and tries to blur and conceal the charges we make. Not all these heads of monasteries are Brahmins. The heads of the Dharmapuram and Thiruvavaduthurai Adheenams are not Brahmins. We do not praise them merely because they are non-Brahmins. Though they are non-Brahmins, do we not condemn them because the country is harmed by them? We have not pleaded on their behalf. We condemn their conduct because they exercise domination in society through their connection with many temples, commit dishonest acts, and because impurity exists in the monasteries.

This meeting takes place amid congestion and during business hours. It was arranged this way so that our ideas might spread among many merchants. We have not had much contact with you. When someone here is discussing the price of five palams of camphor, we will be speaking elsewhere. Because your business hours and our speaking hours coincide, we have been unable to mingle with you.

Because of that distance, you may hold some mistaken ideas about us. I agreed to speak here because I thought it would be good to address you directly at least once. We have not come to deceive you merchants, obtain your votes, and enter the Legislature. We have come to speak in support of a good Bill.

A Chettiar who sells asafoetida will say that he has no curry leaves if you ask him for even one leaf in his shop. You must go inside Kothaval Chavadi to buy curry leaves. Likewise, everything cannot be obtained from a single party.

To make sambar with asafoetida and curry leaves, dal is essential. The dal we buy must be one that cooks properly. We buy a little, cook it, and purchase more only after seeing that it cooks well. If skill is needed even to buy dal and provisions for cooking, consider how much discernment you need in the marketplace of life. Everything required for life cannot be obtained from one party. We must accept some principles from each party.

As merchants, you assess people and examine the goods they require before buying them. In the present Legislature there is no opposition such as existed before. It functions with an overwhelming number of Congressmen. There ought to be no difference of opinion over a Bill introduced with the support of that large majority. Yet some khadi-clad Congressmen oppose it. Though the majority of the people do not support them, *Kalki*, *Swadesamitran*, and *The Hindu* do.

Congressmen formerly called us traitors. We support this Bill brought by Congressmen. Yet some Congressmen oppose it. You will not call them traitors! They possess neither party honour nor discipline, and still many Congressmen listen to them.

Could Varadachari and Vaidyanatha Iyer be expelled from the party? Has it been possible? Is it possible now? Will it ever be possible?

They fear that if this Bill becomes law, all the monasteries will be reformed. We, members of an opposing party whom the Ministry throws into prison, spend the time left to us outside prison speaking for this Bill and, in effect, working for the Ministry. We quarrel with them on many matters. Even so, we support their Bill. Such a wonder would occur in no party politics, whether in India or anywhere in the world.

The Congress party cannot appear wearing the label of the opposition. “Dravidian party” is also only a label. No one should be deceived by labels. Packets marked Scissors cigarettes may contain Akbar Shah cigarettes, and packets marked Akbar Shah may contain Scissors cigarettes. Do not buy goods by looking only at the label; examine what is inside.

Do you imagine that wealthy men such as the Alagappas, Dalmias, and Tatas will always remain in one party? Capitalists may cease to like the Congress tomorrow. As the tax burden on merchants grows, they may hate the Congress and join another party. Many workers may become aggrieved and join another party. No one can say that people will remain forever in the same party.

The Ministry has introduced this Bill with a good intention. Every temple in Tamil Nadu needs reform. But we cannot imagine that every reform we desire will be accomplished by this Bill.

This Bill is a first step, a sign and proof of reform. As the days pass, must they not eventually come to the good reform we advocate? In one temple twenty-seven torches are ordered to be lit. Are all twenty-seven properly lit? After the oil actually poured into them is accounted for, do the remaining two veesais of oil go to the priest's house? Of ten padis of white pongal, do two padis quietly find their way to his home? The Bill is meant to examine such matters. In general, theft must not take place in temples in any form whatever. The Bill has been introduced to prevent it. It will also help discover how many sinful souls and how many virtuous souls occupy the temples.

Temple theft is not a thing of today or yesterday; it has continued since the days of our great-grandfathers. During the reign of the Vijayanagara king Krishnadevaraya, whose rule extended south to Tiruvarur, Nagaraja Nambi, a priest of the Siva temple there, stole two of the sixty-three bronze images of the Nayanmars, melted them down, and sold the metal.

A devotee of Siva in Tiruvarur learned of it and wanted somehow to inform the king, but feared approaching him directly. He had raised a parrot and trained it to sing:

“Krishnadevaraya! Krishnadevaraya!
Once there were sixty-three;
Today two have departed.
The smith cuts them up;
Nagaraja Nambi, who sold them, remains,
O Krishnaraya!”

When Krishnadevaraya later visited Tiruvarur, the devotee sent the parrot to him as a messenger. It repeated exactly what it had been taught. The king investigated, learned the truth, and expelled Nagaraja Nambi beyond the borders of his realm. This song was not composed by an atheist. It appears among the independent verses and may still be found.

There were two poets known as the Twin Poets. One was blind and the other lame. The blind poet carried the lame poet on his shoulders as they travelled from place to place. Once they went to Eengur in Coimbatore district. Driven by hunger, they entered the local Siva temple. The priest performed the worship, covered the offering plate with a cloth, and carried it away without giving them any prasadam. The blind poet appealed to Siva:

“O famed Siva of Eengur, O bearer of the bow,
Is it just that we should remain hungry?”

As though Siva were answering, the lame poet sang:

“Go away, sirs!
Apart from the conch's blare and the leather drum's din,
The mutilated Lord has never seen rice.”

Does this not show that such abuses occurred in temples even then? After careful examination of present-day records, the Chief Minister has discovered theft in eighty-eight temples. How many more have escaped his notice? How many have been concealed? We do not know.

Many of you know that I live in Kanchipuram. At its great Siva temple, the Ekambaranathar temple, Siva was about to proceed in procession on the sacred bull when someone suddenly stole the tuft of silver on the bull's tail. Guards and police were present, yet theft occurred.

Do not imagine theft occurs only in Siva temples. It also occurs in Vaishnavite temples. About five years ago, the prominent Madras lawyer V. V. Srinivasa Iyengar, a trustee of the great Varadaraja Perumal temple in Kanchipuram, presented a white *namam* made of diamonds and a red one made of rubies. It adorned the forehead of Perumal, and devotees gazed at it in rapture.

One Bhagavata may have thought: “If only that *namam* were with us, all our poverty would disappear! Those diamonds would make fitting stones for my Lakshmi's ear ornaments. Are we not the children of God? Does not the father's property belong to the child?” One midnight he climbed the compound wall, entered the temple, placed his hand on Varadaraja Perumal's forehead, and carried away the *namam*. Such thefts take place in many places.

Once this Bill comes into force, the Hindu Religious Endowments Board will ask how many diamonds, emeralds, pearls, other precious stones, and hair ornaments there are. That is precisely why the Bill is needed. It greatly assists in rooting out the remarkable thefts occurring in temples. What is wrong with that? What offence does it contain? Why do Varadachari and Vaidyanatha Iyer oppose it?

Vaidyanatha Iyer knows the law well and is influential in the Madurai region. He has thoroughly studied how law is administered in Australia, Africa, and America, and even knows how various defences might be constructed in the Atlantic Ocean. Should he spend all his intellectual sharpness merely on the Agamas?

Varadacharis and Vaidyanatha Iyers dream: “After this Bill, they will enact many more reforms our people dislike. Our domination will then be endangered. Let us fasten a double bolt now, like the bolt placed against Ottakoothar's song, prevent this Bill from becoming law, and stop all later reform.” Did they not bolt the door against the Sarda Act? Did they not obstruct the Bill against dedicating women as devadasis? Did they not oppose the prohibition of the cruelty of *sati*? They will oppose everything. We are the ones who believe in justice and honesty.

Speaking about this Bill at the Vasantha Mandapam in Triplicane, former minister Dr Subbarayan said, “Do not think the Bill will be spoiled merely because atheists support it.” He calls our wholehearted support harmful. Who says so? Dr Subbarayan. Is the Doctor alone a theist? We are not atheists in the sense he suggests; at the same time, neither is he a theist. At most, he may be called a theist of the year 1949.

Dr Subbarayan is a true atheist. Did he not abandon the customs and observances of his community, disregard caste difference, and marry the beautiful Radhabai for love at the age of twenty-five? Did he not cross the seas and travel to the West?

Devotees burn half a palam of camphor and pray to God for a salary of a thousand rupees. Are we not the ones who say, “Thousands of rupees were spent on the consecration, and my stomach burns because there has been no return”?

They say, “We give God our love; He gives us knowledge and grace.” If God bears hostility toward us because we are atheists, they may curse us: “Let their skulls split while they speak!” Or they may say, “Let their tongues draw back so that they cannot speak,” and surely they would. Say, “Let their hands and feet be paralysed,” and surely they would. It is an easy course. But does anything like that happen anywhere today? Can it happen? It cannot.

A jutka driver who wants you to travel only in his cart will see you about to board a bus and say: “Are you going in this bus? Its tyre is always punctured! You will not arrive quickly. Do not trust that bus and be deceived. Here is the jutka; it will take you there in an instant!” Yet if the same driver has urgent business elsewhere, he will travel on the very bus he condemned. New changes occur in just this fashion.

The people need a sound plan if they are to live. Trade flourishes only when money circulates everywhere. It cannot function properly if money lies locked up in a few places.

Rather than leave five thousand rupees lying idle in a bank, you merchants would use the same five thousand to buy goods and earn a profit. Only then does wealth grow without stagnation. Likewise, why not take the lakhs of rupees lying uselessly locked up in many temples as jewels, lands, vehicles, and crowns, and use them to establish workshops and factories? All forty crore people in India could work and live. The country's economy would grow, and unemployment would disappear.

Is America not a land of wealth? Could Americans not make a golden chariot for Jesus? Could Italy not do so? If the British Empire that ruled us wished, could it not make Jesus a diamond-studded Garuda vehicle? Do their countries lack wealth? Yet it is only in our country that we have golden Garuda vehicles, golden elephants, silver bulls, and temple ornaments set with diamonds, pearls, emeralds, and the nine gems.

The Indian Government intends to mortgage India and borrow from the World Bank. Economic experts from the international bank in America have come for that purpose. The Government has shown them the flowing Cauvery and Godavari, the sky-piercing Himalayas, the Vindhyas, the forests, the land, and all the country's resources, and asked for a loan. The Indian Government stands in the position of borrowing from the world's banks.

Meanwhile, crores of rupees remain locked up in temple jewellery. When people suffer without rice, we strongly condemn the hoarding of hundreds of sacks and their sale on the black market. When poverty rages in the country for want of money, is not the locking away of many crores in temples in God's name another kind of black market?

Would people governed by justice, reason, law, and humanity act so?

Why does Mahavishnu, who reclines upon a living serpent and sleeps upon the sea, need a vehicle? Siva says he needs nothing but a tiger skin. Why, then, does he need a vehicle, a golden elephant, or a silver bull? When a silver chariot proceeds at night, torchbearers are needed. Police are required to prevent theft, superior officers are needed to supervise them, and a minister is needed to inspect the whole affair. All this is demanded in a famine-stricken country. Do churches maintain guards at all four entrances? Is any guard required in their open spaces? No.

Do you know from how far away police must come to provide security for the Garuda Sevai at Kanchipuram? They come from Godavari and other districts. Thousands of devotees arrive for the festival, including people from all eighteen sacred Vaishnavite shrines.

Some devotees come to test the sharpness of their scissors. Evading even the police, they cut jewellery from people's necks and slip quietly away. Is any craft taught in the name of Kanchi Varadaraja? No. Are any schools conducted? No. Why should the temple trustees not use the annual income to run a “Varadaraja Home for the Poor”?

Does not a college now function at Tirupati in the name of Venkatesa Perumal, who is said to end all unending karma? If the money locked away in temples were put to good use, how many new industries could be created?

You merchants need only display a board saying, “Wanted: a fifteen-year-old boy for this shop.” The next day thousands of boys will stand there seeking the job. Unemployment has reached that extent. In this country where industry has declined and economic knowledge is almost nil, think how many people would obtain work if new industries were begun.

When many suffered from unemployment, we used to say the white rulers were responsible. Once there was an enemy whom we could blame for everything. Can we continue to lay the blame that way after August 15? We cannot. The fault lies no longer with the white rulers but with those who govern us.

Iron is now available in our country. Is merely finding it enough? We must use it to establish many good factories. Gold too is available and must be put to proper use. Machinery must be imported from the West. Here itself, as Bharati desired, why should we not heat iron and make the good implements we need?

“Heat and smelt the iron!
Fashion machines from it!”

That is what Bharati said. Should we not find a way for forty crore people to live in well-being?

“Brother, you asked for self-rule; what benefit did you obtain?” Ceylon asks us with a laugh. The Czech maiden, the American woman, and indeed the whole world are prepared to ask: “Has poverty disappeared from your country? Has India once again become the prosperous land it was?”

Tamil kings such as Rajendra and Kulothunga built temples with contributions made from profits earned by people trading overseas. How many thousands must have worked on those temples? How many labourers suffered in sun and rain? There is no mountain at Thanjavur. Across what distances and under what burning sun must they have gathered boulders and black stone? How many sovereigns were spent? How many sculptors laboured night and day?

Devotees in our land have deposited enormous wealth in temples, as though in banks of devotion. If a cheque cannot be converted into cash at an ordinary bank, we think the bank has cheated us and rage at the cashier. Yet things work differently in these banks of devotion.

In ancient times, schools functioned in halls of a hundred or a thousand pillars. Temples contained beautiful gardens and good tanks in which the people could bathe. In the evenings people gathered in those thousand-pillared halls to cultivate their minds and discuss the politics of their time. You know the condition of those halls now. They have fallen so low that someone finding a man sleeping there at midnight might slap him on the back and ask, “Well, brother, was there some celebration here last night?”

There is impurity in the temples. Omandurar introduced this Bill to remove it. Do temple authorities restore the tanks overgrown with moss? They do not. Why should they not use temple funds to establish a hospital for people with leprosy? Why have they not assumed responsibility for protecting the destitute?

Did not the poor call God the friend in danger and protector of orphans? Why should the temple where that protector dwells not shelter orphans?

Has any temple authority ever attended to people with broken arms, no legs, or no sight? You go to hotels and eat refreshments while listening to gentle music in the evening. Afterward, chewing fresh betel leaves, you come out. At the entrance a beggar stands and, whether you have committed any virtue or not, calls, “O virtuous one! Lord of charity! Swami! Master!” and asks for a quarter-anna. Where then is all the song of M. S. that you heard inside? You must forget both the music and the pleasure it produced. Should the condition of these destitute people not change?

Thayumanavar sang:

“I know no desire other than that everyone should live in joy,
O Supreme One!”

You hear singers render it with melody and feeling. What use is their exquisite performance? Who has actually tried to make everyone live happily? I ask you merchants. If Ponnusamy and Duraisamy made the effort, could they not establish at least a reading room or a school?

Many merchants collect charitable offerings and give them to temples. But has even one of you built a secondary school? Have you founded a reading room? You have not.

Many do not know what is sin and what is virtue. Is it enough merely to say there are forty palams to a veesai? Is it enough to verify that the forty-palam stone is correct? Should we not also check whether someone has tampered with it after the Government stamped it?

A festival is celebrated with brilliant gas lamps, dummy-horse dancing, the nadaswaram of Thiruvavaduthurai Rajarathinam Pillai on one side, the Thiruveezhimizhalai Brothers on the other, and fireworks. Three sets of accounts and three accountants are maintained for the Income Tax Office. After succeeding through false accounts, they present a festival to God as a gift. They deceive the Government as well.

Apart from fifty-year-old mothers with their heads covered, do women of thirty really look at God? They say to one another, “Did you see the diamond pendant around God's neck?” At the same moment, a diamond merchant thinks, “How many carats is that diamond? If I supply a similar stone to Suraj Mal, I will receive a good commission!”

If you kept a diary to identify who was virtuous and who was sinful, the person entered as virtuous on the sixth would be entered as sinful on the seventh; the person called sinful on the second would be called virtuous on the eighth.

Three and three make six; four and four make eight; two multiplied by two makes four. Sin and virtue are not fixed conclusions in that fashion.

Once, it is said, a cow rang the bell of justice. The king came and saw it standing in tears. He learned that its calf had died beneath the wheel of the chariot driven by his son, Prince Veedhividangan. “As this cow grieves for its calf, I shall grieve for my only son. That alone is justice,” he said. Manu Needhi Cholan placed his son beneath the chariot wheel and rendered justice to the cow.

Could anyone do that today? Even if someone tried, would the law permit it? Would Section 302 of the law remain idle?

Emperor Sibi is said to have cut flesh from his own body to save a dove fleeing a vulture. That was in those days. Would anyone do it today? If a dove flew toward us, could we even catch it? If we did, would the owner of the pigeon house in Mannadi remain quiet?

Where do we have enough flesh to do as Sibi did? The heads of monasteries might perhaps manage it!

On Sivaratri, a man went into a forest. Darkness fell and he wished to return home. Seeing a tiger behind him, he climbed a nearby bilva tree. Beneath it stood a Sivalinga, though he did not know it. The tiger came to the tree and, intending to kill him, circled it without leaving. The man could not climb down.

Fearing that he might fall asleep in the night, he plucked bilva leaves one by one and dropped them below to stay awake. Nevertheless, he died the next day. When Yama's messengers came to seize his life, Siva's attendants arrived and cried, “Do not touch his life! He is a devotee of Siva,” and took him to Kailasa. The Purana says he attained liberation through leaves he dropped while trying to save his own life, without even knowing there was a Sivalinga below.

Sakkiya Nayanar is said to have obtained liberation by worshipping Siva with stones. Karaikkal Ammaiyar, her body wasted and heart melting, took the form of a spirit and travelled toward Mount Kailasa until her bones were worn away. Yet Siva told her, “Mother, come to Tiruvalangadu.” Which of these paths should one follow to reach God? The Puranas do not state it clearly.

When a poor boy asks us for five or six rupees to pay his school fee, what do we say? “Why do you wander begging like this? What have educated people achieved? Go and work in some shop.” Is that not our answer?

Would the priest who hurries to collect money for a Krittika ablution listen if we said, “You perform ablutions so often; spare us for one Krittika”? They never fail to perform that rite. But they will do nothing to help a poor boy's education.

It would be most fitting if we held that whatever ruins people is sin, and whatever enables them to live is virtue. Why should ascetics and Jeeyars possess so much wealth? Why do those who claim to embrace renunciation need abundant property?

Pattinathar said, “If food does not come in search of the place where I am, I shall go in search of the place where food is.” That is Pattinathar's verse. Ascetics should eat only when someone seeks them out and brings food. That is the character of an ascetic. Why then do the Thiruvavaduthurai pontiffs, who profess renunciation, need wet and dry lands?

Though he was a king's brother, did not Ilango, the younger brother of Cheran Senguttuvan, refuse the burden of rule, live as a true ascetic, and compose the noble *Silappathikaram*?

Why does the Thiruvavaduthurai pontiff need sparkling diamond rings on all ten fingers? Why the water pot in his hand? Why earrings in his ears, and gold glittering within them? Why deceit in his heart? Why singing cuckoos and dancing peacocks beside him? Is this renunciation?

Were ascetics not told simply, “Eat when hungry”? What does that mean? They may consume a vegetable, a ripe fruit, roots and tubers, or even fallen leaves to satisfy hunger. If Buddha renounced his crown, cast aside his kingdom, left his child and beloved wife at midnight, and departed Kapilavastu, that has meaning. If Jains renounced the world, that has meaning. But how can we accept as genuine ascetics those who assume a human form and wear the garb of renunciation merely to deceive the people and live from them?

Forgetting that they were born in the human lineage, these pontiffs are asked, “What is your lineage?” Wearing matted hair, they answer arrogantly, “We belong to the lineage of sacred Kailasa.”

When floods approached the dwellings of the poor, did these Kailasa lineages go there and offer any aid? Did they care for people when plague came? When famine struck, did they distribute the grain in their granaries? They look upon some people and cry, “Sinner! Sinner!” Have they ever offered instruction or help to those whom they call sinners?

Why does a well-fed and overgrown pontiff need a monastery, a water pot, earrings, and doe-eyed women? Why does he wander, intoxicated by a woman's charms, drinking liquor? Why does he enact many diversions in the bedchamber? Ten years ago, did not *Chandrakanta* show us the character of ascetics in Tamil Nadu?

In former times, speaking about pontiffs could result in one's head being shaved. No one can do that now. Even if they did, a thousand heads stand ready to speak.

Do these bloated bellies, which have lost the people's respect, dignity, and compassion, bring any benefit to the country? In earlier days, one could be uprooted in an instant for speaking against pontiffs. That was then. Today every party condemns their conduct, and every party supports this Religious Endowments Protection Bill.

I ask you to examine carefully what is sin and what is virtue, and to live with clarity of reason. With that, I conclude.

---NOTES---
The English preserves Anna's historical terminology and polemical descriptions as source-attributed speech. Terms such as *Brahminism*, *atheist*, *sin*, *virtue*, *sati*, and caste or religious labels are translated in their immediate 1949 rhetorical context and are not modern editorial characterisations.

Traditional weights (*palam*, *veesai*, *padi*), ritual terms (*namam*, *abhishekam*, *Krittika*, *bilva*), institutional names (*Adheenam*, *Jeeyar*), and cited literary or devotional lines are retained or lightly transliterated where a modern substitute would erase the source's cultural reference.

Scan-backed OCR corrections applied before translation: 27 temple torches on Image 5; 5,000 rupees of stock on Image 10; a 15-year-old shop boy, August 15, and Bharati's word எந்திரங்கள் on Image 12; and three malformed readings in the Sivaratri passage on Image 17. The Tamil source remains otherwise verbatim, including period spelling, punctuation, and printing irregularities.
